T5: How Does the System Prevent Hallucinations?
Question: "What safeguards exist to prevent the AI from making things up or citing information that doesn't exist in the contracts?"

Answer
Multi-Layer Anti-Hallucination Architecture
The platform implements 6 distinct layers of hallucination prevention, applied at different stages of the answer pipeline:

Layer 1: Input Guardrails (Pre-Processing)

Provider name resolution via dim_provider_canonical (531 entries + aliases) — rejects unrecognized names before any processing begins
Payer exclusion list blocks questions about competitors (Kaiser, Anthem, Aetna, etc.) being treated as BSC providers
Fake provider detection: if canonical resolution returns zero matches, answer explicitly states "provider not found" rather than generating content
Layer 2: Tool-Level Constraints (During Execution)

SQL queries are restricted to a 13-table whitelist — the LLM physically cannot query tables outside the approved set
DDL/DML regex blocking: CREATE, DROP, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE are pattern-matched and rejected
Catalog prefix enforcement: all generated SQL must target dev_adb.raw.* only
Result limits: SQL_MAX_ROWS = 1000, preventing overwhelming data floods
Layer 3: Evidence Grounding (During Retrieval)

All passages come from vector search with real cosine similarity scores (0.50–0.80 range)
Score threshold of 0.65 filters out weak matches
Passages include provenance metadata: source filename, page estimate, chunk type, amendment order
is_current filter ensures superseded document content is deprioritized
Layer 4: HallucinationGuard (Post-Generation, Zero-Cost) 7 automated checks run on every answer:

Numbers in the answer verified against source data
Provider names verified against session scope
Dates checked for plausibility (1990–2030 range)
Non-BSC provider blocklist (Kaiser, Aetna, etc.)
Universality claims ("all providers have X") validated against SQL counts
Same-provider multi-dimensional claims flagged
Confidence floor: no citations → cannot be HIGH confidence
Layer 5: AnswerGroundingEnforcer (Post-Generation)

Verifies that specific claims in the answer map to specific evidence from tool results
Cross-references cited numbers, dates, and provider names against the raw tool outputs
If grounding ratio falls below threshold → confidence is downgraded
Layer 6: Structural Refusal Patterns

System prompt explicitly instructs: "Say 'I don't know' rather than fabricate"
13 prohibited phrases ("refer to the PDF", "I was unable to determine" when evidence IS present)
False premise correction: if user states incorrect facts, system corrects with data
Out-of-scope detection: questions about claims data, competitor rates, negotiation strategy → explicit refusal with explanation of WHY
Real Example (from stress tests)
Question: "Tell me about Sunrise Valley Medical Center's contract terms"

Response: "I was unable to find 'Sunrise Valley Medical Center' in our contracted provider network. I searched dim_provider_canonical (531 entries) and tbl_genie_provider_profile (306 providers). No match found. I will NOT fabricate contract terms for a provider I cannot verify."

